ctical\n**Individual work · 100 marks · 3 hours from Start test**\n\n1. Run **Check details and countdown**, then **Prepare CSV and libraries**.\n2. In Task 1, write the pandas code to read the file into `raw_data`.\n3. Complete Tasks 1–8 in order. Task 3 produces `cleaned_data`. Save regularly.\n4. Run **Submit your test**, click the button, then open its form and press **Submit**. Wait for confirmation.\n\nThe timer includes setup, interruptions and submission. It stops at your personal deadline or the class cutoff, whichever comes first. To resume, use the same entry link, email and registration number; your timer does not restart.\n\nNo AI-generated answers or collaboration. If a support cell is changed or deleted, use **Edit → Undo**. If submission fails, contact your lecturer before the deadline.\n\n## Coding tasks and time plan\n10 minutes setup · 135 coding · 15 checking · 20 submitting.\n\nUse pandas, matplotlib and ordinary Python. Write only in the answer cells. Run the cells labelled “Run only”; do not edit them.\n\nKeep function names and return keys unchanged. The supplied first line turns input records into a DataFrame. Calculate from that input; the grader checks other values too. Later functions can earn marks even if cleaning is unfinished.\n"}, "CML3112|TEST1|A": {"notebook": "{\"cells\": [{\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# CML3112 Test 1 — Take-home practical\\n\", \"**Individual work · 100 marks · 3 hours from Start test**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"1. Run **Check details and countdown**, then **Prepare CSV and libraries**.\\n\", \"2. In Task 1, write the pandas code to read the file into `raw_data`.\\n\", \"3. Complete Tasks 1–8 in order. Task 3 produces `cleaned_data`. Save regularly.\\n\", \"4. Run **Submit your test**, click the button, then open its form and press **Submit**. Wait for confirmation.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The timer includes setup, interruptions and submission. It stops at your personal deadline or the class cutoff, whichever comes first. To resume, use the same entry link, email and registration number; your timer does not restart.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"No AI-generated answers or collaboration. If a support cell is changed or deleted, use **Edit → Undo**. If submission fails, contact your lecturer before the deadline.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## Coding tasks and time plan\\n\", \"10 minutes setup · 135 coding · 15 checking · 20 submitting.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Use pandas, matplotlib and ordinary Python. Write only in the answer cells. Run the cells labelled “Run only”; do not edit them.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Keep function names and return keys unchanged. The supplied first line turns input records into a DataFrame. Calculate from that input; the grader checks other values too. Later functions can earn marks even if cleaning is unfinished.\\n\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"FULL_NAME = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"EMAIL = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"STUDENT_ID = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"COHORT = \\\"CML3112\\\"\\n\", \"TEST_ID = \\\"TEST1\\\"\\n\", \"VARIANT = \\\"A\\\"\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Understand the supplied CSV\\n\", \"Each row is one synthetic observation of **building cooling load**. This is a practice engineering dataset, not evidence for a real safety decision.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"| Column | Meaning |\\n\", \"|---|---|\\n\", \"| record_id | Observation identifier, not time; a repeated ID is a duplicate |\\n\", \"| equipment_id | Equipment label; many observations can belong to the same equipment |\\n\", \"| load_pct | Load percentage; valid values are 0–100 inclusive |\\n\", \"| ambient_c | Ambient temperature, degrees Celsius |\\n\", \"| target | building cooling load, in kW |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Blank cells become missing values in pandas. All tasks use this same CSV. Tasks 1–2 use RAW data; Task 3 cleans it; Tasks 4–8 use CLEANED data.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The supplied `records_from_table` helper converts a table into a list of row dictionaries. You do not need to write or change it. Each function's supplied first line converts those rows back into a DataFrame. This allows the grader to check different data without changing your code.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"cellView\": \"form\", \"tags\": [\"iuea-support\"]}, \"source\": [\"#@title Run only — prepare CSV and libraries\\n\", \"import pandas as pd\\n\", \"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\", \"\\n\", \"def records_from_table(table):\\n\", \"    objects = table.astype(object)\\n\", \"    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict(\\\"records\\\")\\n\", \"\\n\", \"TARGET_UNIT = 'kW'\\n\", \"print(\\\"Setup ready. Continue to Task 1.\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 1: EDA before cleaning — 10 marks · about 15 minutes\\n\", \"\\n\", \"First read `engineering_data.csv` with pandas, using `file_path`, and store the table in `raw_data` (2 marks). The file is in the current folder, so its name is the relative file path.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Then use the original data, including its errors. Calculate and return:\\n\", \"- `preview`: the first five rows as a DataFrame (2 marks).\\n\", \"- `rows` and `columns`: the two dimensions (2 marks together).\\n\", \"- `missing`: missing-value count for each column as a Series (2 marks).\\n\", \"- `duplicates`: repeated record IDs after the first occurrence (2 marks).\\n\", \"The return dictionary is supplied. Replace each `None` with your calculation. Do not clean this table. The supplied run cell also calls `info()` to show data types and recorded counts, and `describe()` to show numeric summaries; these inspection commands carry no extra marks.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-read-csv\"]}, \"source\": [\"file_path = \\\"engineering_data.csv\\\"\\n\", \"# Write your pandas CSV-reading statement below. Store the result in 