ames, argument order and dictionary keys exactly as printed. Keep full numerical precision.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"import pandas as pd\\n\", \"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\", \"\\n\", \"def records_from_table(table):\\n\", \"    objects = table.astype(object)\\n\", \"    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict(\\\"records\\\")\\n\", \"\\n\", \"raw_table = pd.read_csv(\\\"engineering_data.csv\\\")\\n\", \"records = records_from_table(raw_table)\\n\", \"print(raw_table.head())\\n\", \"print(\\\"Rows:\\\", len(raw_table))\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Supplied preparation — not graded\\n\", \"Keep the first copy of each record ID, exclude rows without a target and rows outside the valid load range. These are fixed row rules, not learned model parameters. **Do not fill missing ambient temperatures here.** Your model function must learn its median only from training records using `SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\")`, followed by the model in a scikit-learn pipeline. Features are `load_pct` then `ambient_c`, in that order. The target must not be a feature.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"eligible = raw_table.drop_duplicates(\\\"record_id\\\").copy()\\n\", \"eligible = eligible[eligible[\\\"target\\\"].notna() & eligible[\\\"load_pct\\\"].between(0, 100)].copy()\\n\", \"records = records_from_table(eligible)\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 1: Split — 20 marks\\n\", \"`group_split(records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Assign whole equipment groups: E1–E4 to train, E5 to validation, E6 to test. Return train, validation and test lists of record_id in input order. Do not use target values to choose membership.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def group_split(records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 2: Regression — 30 marks\\n\", \"`predict_regression(training_records, feature_records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Fit a training-mean baseline, linear regression and a decision tree (max_depth=4, random_state=17) using load_pct and ambient_c only. Return three prediction lists under baseline, linear and tree. Feature records have no target. Fit preprocessing only on training data. Use SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\") fitted on training records only, separately in each model pipeline. The baseline is the mean training target. Preserve feature order: load_pct, ambient_c.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def predict_regression(training_records, feature_records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 3: Errors — 20 marks\\n\", \"`regression_errors(actual, predicted)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Return mae and rmse, calculated over the same paired observations, with full precision.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def regression_errors(actual, predicted):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Structured interpretation — 30 marks\\n\", \"Choose exactly one option per question. Each is worth 5 marks. These questions use the stated scenarios; do not replace their supplied values with your CSV results.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D1. Why keep all records for an equipment ID in one partition?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: To make all partition sizes equal. | B: To select equipment using target values. | C: To avoid evaluating on equipment already represented during fitting.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D2. Which records may determine the median used to fill missing model inputs?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Training records only; reuse those medians for validation and test. | B: All records before splitting. | C: Test records because they are the newest.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D3. Supplied validation MAE: baseline 4.0, linear 1.2, tree 1.8 target units. Select by smallest validation MAE.**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Baseline. | B: Linear. | C: Tree.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D4. When may E6 test targets be used?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: While choosing the model with the smallest error. | B: To fit the imputer. | C: Once for final evaluation after model selection is fixed.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D5. A few predictions have very large errors. Why can RMSE exceed MAE?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Squaring errors gives larger errors more influence. | B: RMSE is a percentage. | C: MAE ignores every large error.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D6. A model performs well on this synthetic dataset. What is the next justified step?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Deploy it as a safety system immediately. | B: Evaluate representative measured data and operating conditions before considering deployment. | C: Claim it works on every engineering system.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"DECISIONS = {\\\"D1\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D2\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D3\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D4\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D5\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D6\\\": \\\"\\\"}\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Run your functions on the supplied CSV\\n\", \"Run this supplied cell after implementing the tasks. It calls your functions in the required order and displays their results. This is a check of your work, not an extra task or additional marks. If a function is unfinished, return to that task. Function checks are independent: an unfinished earlier function does not prevent the grader checking later functions on its own inputs.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"partitions = group_split(records)\\n\", \"training = [r for r in records if r[\\\"record_id\\\"] in partitions[\\\"train\\\"]]\\n\", \"validation = [r for r in records if r[\\\"record_id\\\"] in partitions[\\\"validation\\\"]]\\n\", \"features = [{k: v for k, v in r.items() if k != \\\"target\\\"} for r in validation]\\n\", \"predictions = predict_regression(training, features)\\n\", \"actual = [r[\\\"target\\\"] for r in validation]\\n\", \"for name, predicted in predictions.items():\\n\", \"    print(name, regression_errors(actual, predicted))\\n\", \"# E6 is reserved for final evaluation; do not use it to select a model.\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Submit your test\\n\", \"Run the cell below and click **Submit test**. Open the generated form link, check your details and press **Submit** there. Use your own Google account in both Colab and the form. Wait for the form confirmation. If anyt